# Trivy CVE exemptions — narrow, per-CVE, with written reason + upgrade plan.
# Each entry MUST include the CVE id, the affected package path, the reason
# the exemption is safe today, and the trigger that retires the exemption.
#
# Adding entries here is a structural escape hatch for the Trivy gate. Use
# sparingly: prefer fixing the dep over ignoring it.
#
# ── Current exemptions: NONE ──
#
# The gate runs unexempted. Keep it that way if you can: an entry here is a
# CVE the image ships and nobody is fixing.
#
# ── Historical note (2026-10-01): nine entries retired at once ──
#
# Every exemption this file carried was justified by ONE thing — the npm CLI
# inside the runtime image — and #3035 deleted it:
#
#   CVE-2026-12151, CVE-2026-9679, CVE-2026-6733, CVE-2026-11525  (undici)
#   CVE-2026-53655, CVE-2026-73566                                (tar)
#   CVE-2026-14257, CVE-2026-69152                                (brace-expansion)
#   CVE-2026-69192                                                (ip-address)
#
# Each said the affected copy lived in `/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm`'s
# vendored tree and was unreachable because the server runs `next start` and
# never invokes npm. #3035 stopped relying on the "unreachable" half: the
# Dockerfile now `rm -rf`s the npm CLI and asserts `! command -v npm` at build
# time, so the vulnerable copies are not in the image to be reached. Three of
# the entries (the brace-expansion pair and ip-address) justified themselves by
# citing a `npm install -g npm@12.0.1` line that #3035 also deleted, so their
# stated reasoning pointed at a Dockerfile that no longer existed.
#
# Verified before removal, on the live production container rather than from the
# alert list — `.trivyignore` filters BEFORE the SARIF upload, so an exempted
# CVE and an absent one look identical from the Security tab:
#
#   /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm          ABSENT
#   undici / brace-expansion / ip-address / tar
#     directories outside /app/node_modules  0
#   /usr/local/lib/node_modules (control)    PRESENT
#
# So all four packages now exist only in the application's own runtime tree,
# where the lockfile and the package.json `overrides` pin patched versions.
#
# Retiring these makes the gate STRICTER, which is the safe direction to be
# wrong in: if any copy survives somewhere this probe did not look, Trivy
# blocks the publish and says which package, instead of waving it through.
#
# ── If a future advisory needs an exemption, include ──
#
#   - the CVE id
#   - the affected package path
#   - WHY the exemption is safe (reachability / blast radius)
#   - the trigger that retires it (specific dep bump, base-image refresh,
#     deprecation of the vulnerable feature)
#
# And prefer a premise a test can hold up over one a reader has to trust:
# `tests/guards/runner-never-invokes-npm.test.ts` was written to enforce the
# reachability claim behind CVE-2026-73566 rather than assert it in prose.
